---
import NoteLayout from '@/layouts/note-layout.astro'
import RelatedNotes from '@/components/related-notes.astro'
import { RESCUE_AUDIT, formatUsd } from '@/lib/pricing'

const codeInstall = `pip install "langfuse>=4.7"

export LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY="pk-lf-..."
export LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY="sk-lf-..."
export LANGFUSE_BASE_URL="https://cloud.langfuse.com"  # or your self-hosted URL`

const codeTrace = `from langfuse import observe, propagate_attributes
from langfuse.openai import openai  # drop-in client, every model call is logged

@observe(as_type="tool")
def lookup_order(order_id: str) -> dict:
    return orders_api.get(order_id)

@observe(as_type="agent")
def support_agent(question: str, user_id: str, chat_id: str) -> str:
    # Enter this before any child step starts, so every observation carries it
    with propagate_attributes(user_id=user_id, session_id=chat_id):
        return run_agent_loop(question, tools=[lookup_order])`

const codeGate = `from langfuse import Evaluation, RegressionError, RunnerContext

THRESHOLD = 0.9

def task(*, item, **kwargs):
    return support_agent(item.input["question"], user_id="ci", chat_id=item.id)

def correct_status(*, output, expected_output, **kwargs):
    ok = expected_output["status"] in output
    return Evaluation(name="correct_status", value=1.0 if ok else 0.0)

def experiment(context: RunnerContext):
    result = context.run_experiment(
        name="support-agent",
        task=task,
        evaluators=[correct_status],
    )
    scores = [
        e.value
        for r in result.item_results
        for e in r.evaluations
        if e.name == "correct_status"
    ]
    avg = sum(scores) / len(scores) if scores else 0.0
    if avg < THRESHOLD:
        raise RegressionError(
            result=result, metric="correct_status", value=avg, threshold=THRESHOLD
        )
    return result`

// Zero failures in n runs rules out, at 95% confidence, any failure rate above 1 - 0.05^(1/n).
const cleanRuns = [
	[5, '45%'],
	[20, '14%'],
	[59, '5%'],
	[300, '1%'],
]

const link = 'underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground'
const ext = { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer', class: link }
---

<NoteLayout
	title="Langfuse: Stop Guessing Whether Your AI Agent Works | Ilzam"
	description="Five passing prompts tell you almost nothing about an AI agent. The reliability math, how Langfuse stores traces in ClickHouse, and evals that can fail a build."
>
	<h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-light leading-tight tracking-tight md:text-4xl">
		Stop Playing Tebak-Tebak Berhadiah With Your AI Agent
	</h1>

	<blockquote class="mt-6">
		<p class="text-sm font-medium">TL;DR</p>
		<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			You built an agent or a harness, tried it on five prompts, and shipped it because it felt right. That's tebak-tebak berhadiah, Indonesian for a
			guessing game with a prize. The math is unkind: five clean runs only rule out a failure rate above 45%. This note covers why agents slip past
			normal testing, how <strong>Langfuse</strong> ingests and stores traces (and why v4 rebuilt its data model around observations), how to build
			evaluators you can trust, and a CI gate that catches real regressions without failing on noise.
		</p>
	</blockquote>

	<section class="mt-10">
		<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
			Someone builds an AI agent. It reads a customer's question, calls a couple of tools, searches a knowledge base, and writes an answer. They try it
			on five or six questions they thought of, every answer looks good, and it goes live.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			A week later a customer complains that the bot said their order had shipped when it hadn't. The developer adds a sentence to the prompt, "only
			report order status from the lookup tool", reruns the same five questions, and they still pass. Ship it.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Did that fix it? Did it break something else? How often was it happening in the first place? Nobody knows. In Indonesia we call this
			<em>tebak-tebak berhadiah</em>: the guessing game at a 17 Agustus neighborhood party, where someone shouts an answer and hopes for a prize. It's
			a fine way to spend an afternoon in a kampung. As a release process, it's how you lose customers without ever learning why.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The fix is engineering, and most of it is unglamorous: counting, sampling, and storing the right data in the right shape. Langfuse happens to be
			a good place to see all of it, because its own architecture went through the same lessons.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Five passing prompts rule out almost nothing</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Treat each run of your agent as a trial that comes out correct with some unknown probability. Your five test prompts are a sample of that
			distribution, and a sample of five is tiny.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Say the agent is wrong one time in five. The chance that all five of your tries pass anyway is 0.8<sup>5</sup>, about 33%. A third of the time,
			your test session reports a clean bill of health on an agent that fails 20% of real requests.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Now ask what a clean streak actually proves. With zero failures in <em>n</em> runs, the highest failure rate you can rule out at 95% confidence is
			1 &minus; 0.05<sup>1/<em>n</em></sup>:
		</p>
		<div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
			<table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
				<thead>
					<tr class="border-b">
						<th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Clean runs in a row</th>
						<th class="py-3 font-medium">Failure rates you can rule out (95%)</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
					{
						cleanRuns.map(([runs, rate]) => (
							<tr class="border-b">
								<td class="py-3 pr-4 text-foreground">{runs}</td>
								<td class="py-3">Above {rate}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The bottom rows follow the rule of three: zero failures in <em>n</em> runs puts the true rate below roughly 3/<em>n</em>. To claim your agent
			fails less than 5% of the time, you need about 60 clean runs on realistic inputs, which nobody types by hand.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Agents compound the problem because a run is a chain of decisions, and errors multiply along it. If each step (pick a tool, fill in its
			arguments, read the result correctly) is right 95% of the time, a 10-step run is fully right 0.95<sup>10</sup>, about 60% of the time.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Consistency matters as much as capability. The τ-bench paper [1] measures pass<sup>k</sup>, the probability that an agent succeeds on all
			<em>k</em> attempts at the same task. GPT-4o solved under half of its tasks, and its pass<sup>8</sup> in the retail domain was under 25%. A
			customer who asks the same thing twice and gets two different answers has just measured your pass<sup>2</sup>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Why your usual tooling doesn't see it</h2>
		<div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
			<div class="rounded-lg bg-muted/50 p-5">
				<p class="font-medium">Temperature 0 doesn't make it deterministic</p>
				<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
					Identical requests at temperature 0 can still produce different outputs. Inference servers batch requests from many users together, and
					common GPU kernels give slightly different floating-point results depending on batch size, so your output depends on how many other people
					were calling the API at that moment [2]. One different token early in a run can send an agent down a different path.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="rounded-lg bg-muted/50 p-5">
				<p class="font-medium">Failures return 200 OK</p>
				<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
					APM tools watch status codes, latency, and exceptions. An agent failure is semantic. The request returns 200 in four seconds, the error
					tracker stays quiet, and the customer reads a confident, wrong answer. Stack traces tell you whether code ran, and that's all they tell you.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="rounded-lg bg-muted/50 p-5">
				<p class="font-medium">The prompt is global state</p>
				<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
					Every request reads the same system prompt, so editing it is like changing a global variable every code path depends on. The sentence that
					fixes order-status answers also changes refund answers. In my
					<a href="/notes/ai-security-scanner-upstash-box/" class={link}>AI security scanner</a>, the biggest quality jump came from one line, "Only
					flag real, concrete issues." A line that cuts noise that well can also cut real findings, and only a measurement over many cases tells you
					which one happened.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="rounded-lg bg-muted/50 p-5">
				<p class="font-medium">Token cost grows with the square of the step count</p>
				<p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
					Each step of the loop resends the conversation so far, every earlier tool result included. Start with 2,000 tokens of context, add 1,000
					tokens of tool output per step, and a 5-step run sends 20,000 input tokens while a 25-step run sends 350,000. Five times the steps costs
					more than 17 times the tokens. Prompt caching discounts the repeated prefix, but the context window still fills. When I
					<a href="/notes/gitnexus-codebase-rag-benchmark/" class={link}>benchmarked GitNexus against Claude Code's native exploration</a>, cache
					reads were 88 to 96% of all tokens, and the cheaper run won by needing fewer steps: about 12 tool calls against 38.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">How Langfuse is built, and why it changes your instrumentation</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Langfuse is an open-source platform for tracing and evaluating LLM applications. The main repository is MIT licensed apart from its enterprise
			folders, and ClickHouse acquired the company in January 2026 with no planned license changes [3]. For an engineer, the plumbing is the
			interesting part, because a few of its design decisions dictate how you should write your instrumentation.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">The SDK is OpenTelemetry underneath</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The Python and JS/TS SDKs are built on OpenTelemetry, and an observation is an OTel span. The <code>@observe</code> decorator opens a span and
			puts it in the OTel context (contextvars, in Python), so any observed function called inside it becomes a child automatically, through nested
			calls and async tasks. Finished spans are queued in memory and exported in batches by a background thread, which keeps tracing off your request
			path.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Batching has a price. A short-lived process can exit before the exporter runs: a serverless runtime may freeze the process right after the
			response, and a CLI or cron job simply ends. Call <code>langfuse.flush()</code> before that point or the last traces never arrive.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">Ingestion is a queue, so reads lag writes</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Langfuse v2 did all of its work inside the ingestion request. Each event meant looking up the existing row, tokenizing the prompt and completion,
			computing cost, and upserting into Postgres. Bursty batch traffic exhausted Postgres IOPS and burned CPU on tokenization, and ingestion latency
			spiked to 50 seconds in the summer of 2023 [4].
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			v3 split the path in two. The API checks auth and body format, writes the raw events to S3, and pushes a reference onto a Redis queue. A worker
			pulls the reference, loads every event for that object, computes the final state, and inserts it into ClickHouse without reading from ClickHouse
			first, which sidesteps read-after-write races. Raw events in S3 can be replayed after a failure, and Redis only carries pointers, which keeps
			it clear of its network limits.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The consequence for you is eventual consistency. A trace appears seconds after the request ends, so a test that writes a trace and immediately
			reads it back through the API can fail for reasons unrelated to your code.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">v4 rebuilt storage around observations</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			In v3, traces and observations lived in separate ClickHouse tables on <code>ReplacingMergeTree</code>. An update was written as a new full row,
			duplicates only disappeared when background merges got around to them, and queries had to deduplicate at read time with <code>FINAL</code> or
			<code>LIMIT BY</code>. Any question like cost per user needed a join between the two tables. Data processed grew 19x over a year, and the largest
			projects could only load a few days of dashboard data [5].
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			v4 replaces this with one wide, mostly immutable observations table. OTel spans are immutable once they end, and roughly 60% of Langfuse Cloud
			observations already arrive over the OTel endpoint, so most rows never need a merge. <code>trace_id</code> becomes a correlation column, like
			<code>session_id</code>. Attributes such as <code>user_id</code>, <code>session_id</code>, and tags are written onto every observation at
			ingestion, so cost per user becomes a column filter plus a <code>GROUP BY</code>, with no join [5].
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			It's the same lesson as my
			<a href="/notes/elasticsearch-prod-is-not-a-youtube-tutorial/" class={link}>Elasticsearch post-mortem</a>: shape storage for the queries you
			actually run, and make every write carry what those queries need.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">What that means for your code</h3>
		<ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Set attributes at the top of the request.</strong> <code>propagate_attributes</code> applies
				<code>user_id</code>, <code>session_id</code>, and tags to the current observation and its children inside its scope [6]. Steps that start
				before you enter it won't carry them, and filters on those attributes won't find those steps.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Upgrade the SDK.</strong> Real-time data on v4 needs Python SDK 4.7.0 or JS/TS SDK 5.4.0 and up, and older
				SDKs can lag by up to 15 minutes. Langfuse Cloud deprecates the v3 endpoints on November 16, 2026, and trace-level LLM judges must move to
				observations by then [7].
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Don't assert on fresh writes.</strong> Read your own traces back with a retry, or assert on the experiment
				result object your script already holds.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Instrumenting an agent</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Three environment variables, the drop-in OpenAI client so every model call gets logged with model, tokens, and cost, and a decorator on your own
			functions so they show up as typed steps:
		</p>
		<pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-muted p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code set:text={codeInstall} /></pre>
		<pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-muted p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code set:text={codeTrace} /></pre>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Use the observation types. Langfuse has ten, including <code>agent</code>, <code>tool</code>, <code>retriever</code>,
			<code>generation</code>, and <code>guardrail</code> [8], and a typed trace reads like your design instead of a column of anonymous spans. Since
			it's all OTel, a request that crosses services (an API that enqueues a job for a worker) can stay one trace if you pass the W3C trace context
			along with the job.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Harnesses need this as much as chatbots do. If you run agents inside sandboxes (I built
			<a href="/work/kotakpasir-agent-sandboxes/" class={link}>Kotak Pasir</a> for that), the sandbox decides what the agent is allowed to do. The trace
			records what it actually tried.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Evals: push each check down to the cheapest layer that can decide it</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			A score is a number, boolean, category, or text note attached to an observation. Where scores come from matters far more than the dashboard they
			land on. I sort evaluators by cost and variance, and move every check as far down that list as it will go.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">Code evaluators: zero variance, almost zero cost</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Anything code can decide should be decided by code. For agents, the most useful checks look at the trajectory as well as the final text:
		</p>
		<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			<li>the lookup tool ran before the answer stated an order status</li>
			<li>tool arguments validate against the tool's schema</li>
			<li>the run finished inside its step budget</li>
			<li>no tool was called twice in a row with identical arguments, the usual sign of a stuck loop</li>
			<li>the output parses as the JSON your frontend expects</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Langfuse runs Python or TypeScript code evaluators on live observations, with a 2-second limit, no network access, and no third-party packages
			[9]. An evaluator can only check what the observation carries, so if a check needs the tool's raw output or the list of steps, record it on the
			observation the evaluator runs against. Checks that need the whole trace are easier in experiments, where your evaluator is ordinary code.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">LLM judges: useful, biased, and in need of calibration</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			For questions code can't settle, like whether an answer stayed faithful to the retrieved documents, a second model grades the output against
			your rubric. Langfuse runs judges on observations, with filters on type, name, metadata, user, session, or tags, and a sampling rate to cap cost
			[10].
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			A judge is a model with failure modes of its own. The MT-Bench paper [11] documents position bias (favoring whichever answer comes first),
			verbosity bias (favoring longer answers), and self-enhancement bias (favoring output from its own model family). What helps in practice: a judge
			from a different model family than the agent, binary or small categorical labels instead of 1-to-10 scales, and a rubric that spells out
			concrete failure conditions.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Then calibrate it. Label a set of observations yourself and compare. Use Cohen's kappa rather than raw percent agreement, because raw agreement
			flatters a judge on imbalanced data. If 90% of your agent's answers are good, a judge that always says "good" agrees with you 90% of the time and
			has a kappa of exactly 0. Langfuse has a human vs AI annotation agreement view for this comparison. Skip calibration and you've traded
			tebak-tebak berhadiah for tebak-tebak berhadiah with charts.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold">Humans and users: the ground truth</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Annotation queues give you or a domain expert a review inbox, and those labels become the reference set your judge is calibrated against. User
			feedback, thumbs up or down from the browser SDK or your server, is noisy and skews toward unhappy users. It's still the only signal that comes
			from the people you're building for, and a thumbs down is the best pointer to a trace worth reading.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Experiments and a CI gate that doesn't flake</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Every low-scored trace is a real input your agent got wrong, which beats anything you'd invent at your desk as test material. Add it to a
			dataset, from the trace view or through the SDK, with an expected output where you have one. An experiment runs your current code over the
			dataset and scores each item with the same evaluators, so two prompt versions, two models, or two branches can be compared item by item.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Langfuse ships a GitHub Action, <code>langfuse/experiment-action</code>, that runs an experiment script on pull requests. The script raises
			<code>RegressionError</code> when a score falls below your threshold, the job fails, and the action can post the scores as a PR comment. It needs
			Python SDK 4.6.0 or JS SDK 5.3.0 and up [12].
		</p>
		<pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-muted p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed"><code set:text={codeGate} /></pre>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			The hard part is that threshold, because the score is a sample too. With 50 items and a true pass rate of 90%, the standard error is
			&radic;(0.9 &times; 0.1 / 50), about 4.2 points, so the 95% interval is roughly &plusmn;8 points. An unchanged agent can score 86% on a bad day,
			and a fixed 0.9 threshold on a 50-item set will fail random pull requests until the team learns to rerun the job and ignore it. To keep the gate
			honest:
		</p>
		<ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Size the dataset for the change you want to catch.</strong> At 200 items the same interval narrows to about
				&plusmn;4 points.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Compare against the main branch, not a constant.</strong> Score main on the same dataset and fail when the
				drop is larger than the noise.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong class="text-foreground">Give critical cases their own gate.</strong> An average can hide a case you just broke, so the Langfuse docs
				recommend checking each case against a reviewed baseline. Since a single run can flip on nondeterminism alone, run those cases <em>k</em> times
				and require pass<sup>k</sup>.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Running it yourself</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Self-hosted Langfuse is two containers, web and worker, on top of Postgres, ClickHouse, Redis or Valkey, and S3-compatible storage, with a Docker
			Compose file for a local trial [13]. Self-hosted v4 is generally available, and v3 gets security patches through January 2027 [7]. Traces hold
			your prompts, your users' messages, and whatever your tools returned, so if a contract or a regulator says that data stays on your servers, this
			is the path.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			ClickHouse is the component that needs real operations work: disk, retention, merges. During the v4 migration, Langfuse's own team found
			partitions with around 1,000 parts where 150 to 200 is typical, and nodes learning about new data 25 minutes after insertion [5]. Budget for that,
			or use the cloud. The free Hobby plan gives 50,000 units a month, and a unit is any data point you send (the trace, each step, each score), so a
			10-step run with two scores costs about 13 units, a little under 4,000 runs a month [14].
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">When this is overkill</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			In an earlier note I called a lot of AI tooling
			<a href="/notes/process-theater-vs-shipping-code/" class={link}>cosplay</a>, evaluation harnesses included, when they get bolted onto tasks
			that don't need them. I still think that, so here's where the line sits.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			If you use AI to write code, say Claude Code building five CRUD screens, you don't need Langfuse. The output is code, and you verify it the way
			you verify any code: read the diff, run the tests. Once it's merged, the model is out of the picture.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Langfuse earns its place when the model runs in production and its answers are the product: a support agent, a search over your documents, a
			classifier that routes tickets, a harness that runs agents for your customers. There every request is a fresh draw from the distribution, and
			nobody can review them all by hand.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			Match the setup to the stage. With ten beta users, tracing alone is enough, and you'll read every trace anyway. Add evaluators once traffic
			outgrows your reading time. Add datasets and the CI gate when prompts change weekly and a silent regression would cost you a customer.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The setup I'd start with</h2>
		<ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			<li>
				Install Python SDK 4.7.0 or JS/TS SDK 5.4.0 or newer. Trace every request, set <code>user_id</code> and <code>session_id</code> at the top with
				<code>propagate_attributes</code>, and call <code>flush()</code> in anything short-lived.
			</li>
			<li>Write code evaluators for trajectory failures: step budget, required tool calls, schema-valid arguments, parseable output.</li>
			<li>
				Hand-label 100 observations. Then add one LLM judge for the quality question code can't answer, and check its kappa against your labels before
				you trust it.
			</li>
			<li>Send every thumbs down and every low score to a dataset.</li>
			<li>
				Gate pull requests that touch prompts, tools, or model settings on an experiment, with a threshold set from measured noise and a separate
				pass<sup>k</sup> gate for critical cases.
			</li>
			<li>Alert on steps per run and cost per run. A loop that stops terminating shows up there before it shows up on the invoice.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12">
		<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The takeaway</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
			An agent that passes the five questions you tried has a failure rate somewhere below 45%. That's the honest reading of the evidence. Langfuse
			leaves the model exactly as smart as it was. What it gives you is evidence: traces that explain each failure, and scores with known error bars
			that can block a regression before a customer finds it. With both in place, shipping a prompt change stops being a quiz show.
		</p>
	</section>

